+# Creative Production Wave 11 — UV, Material Stack and Texture Projection
+
+Wave 11 connects precision mesh geometry to non-destructive texture/material production while keeping geometry, UV state and material state separately inspectable.
+
+## Exact capability growth
+
+Wave 11 adds **53 executable hands** in a separate registry:
+
+| family | hands |
+| --- | ---: |
+| `uv-production` | 20 |
+| `material-stack` | 11 |
+| `material-anchor` | 7 |
+| `material-generator` | 9 |
+| `texture-projection` | 6 |
+| **total** | **53** |
+
+With the previously merged body this candidate exposes:
+
+- **441 executable creative hands**
+- **448 callable creative recipes**
+
+The registry joins the existing dynamic Creative Hands service. Creative Flow itself is unchanged.
+
+## Per-corner UV layout
+
+`axm.precision-uv-layout/v1` is bound to one exact `axm.precision-mesh/v1` digest and stores UV coordinates per triangle corner rather than forcing discontinuous UV seams into the indexed geometry.
+
+This allows the machine to represent real UV seams while the geometry remains one canonical mesh.
+
+Executable UV operations include:
+
+- create a layout from the mesh's current UVs or explicit corner UVs;
+- mark/unmark/clear exact mesh-edge seams;
+- mark indexed boundary edges as seams;
+- derive sharp seams from indexed face-normal angle;
+- discover seam-separated UV islands;
+- inspect one island;
+- translate, scale, rotate and integer-UDIM-shift one island;
+- dominant-axis planar projection for one island;
+- project all islands;
+- deterministic grid packing with padding;
+- texel-density measurement and scaling;
+- per-triangle relative area-distortion evidence;
+- exact positive-area triangle overlap audit for bounded meshes;
+- explicit materialization into a mesh whose vertices are split only where one indexed geometry vertex needs different corner UVs.
+
+### UV truth boundary
+
+This is not an LSCM, ABF, conformal or angle-preserving unwrap implementation. `project-island` selects the dominant geometry axis and performs normalized planar projection. It is useful, deterministic and inspectable, but it must not be represented as a production conformal unwrap.
+
+Packing is deterministic grid-cell packing, not a bin-packing/rotation optimizer. It prioritizes reproducibility and no overlap over maximum texture occupancy.
+
+Texel-density evidence is area based. Distortion evidence reports relative triangle area scaling; it does not yet measure angular/conformal distortion.
+
+The overlap audit computes positive-area UV triangle intersections and fails closed above 2,000 faces because the current implementation is quadratic.
+
+`materialize-split-mesh` intentionally changes indexed topology where UV discontinuities require duplicate vertices. The source geometry remains unchanged; the materialized mesh records the originating UV layout digest.
+
+## Non-destructive material stacks
+
+`axm.precision-material-stack/v1` wraps the existing `axm.precision-material/v1` channel body with ordered layers.
+
+Each layer has:
+
+- stable ID;
+- one or more precision-raster channels;
+- optional precision mask;
+- one of the existing deterministic blend modes;
+- bounded opacity;
+- enabled/disabled state;
+- explicit metadata.
+
+Executable stack operations include create/add/remove/duplicate/move, opacity/blend/mask/channel editing, enable/disable and deterministic flattening back into the existing precision-material body.
+
+Flattening is real pixel execution through the existing alpha-correct precision compositor. Missing destination channels begin from a deterministic black opaque raster before a new channel layer is composited.
+
+## Anchors
+
+Anchors are explicit image snapshots, not invisible live graph references.
+
+An anchor records:
+
+- one exact image snapshot;
+- whether it came from a flattened stack or a named layer/channel;
+- the source stack/material evidence at capture time.
+
+Anchors can produce luminance, edge or named-channel masks; copy their snapshot into another layer channel; or drive a layer mask.
+
+Changing a layer later does **not** silently rewrite an existing anchor. A fresh anchor must be created when fresh source state is intended.
+
+## Material generators
+
+The stack can add:
+
+- constant channel fills;
+- the existing deterministic procedural raster generators;
+- decal layers;
+- wear, dust and scratch masks derived from the current flattened material;
+- those generated masks directly onto a selected layer.
+
+These reuse the existing material/raster engines instead of introducing a parallel texture generator.
+
+Wear remains an edge-derived mask from height/normal/base-colour evidence. Dust and scratches are bounded deterministic procedural masks. They are not physically simulated aging.
+
+## Geometry-to-texture projection
+
+The texture projection engine rasterizes mesh triangles through the per-corner UV layout with a bounded barycentric rasterizer.
+
+It executes:
+
+- normalized world-position map;
+- interpolated world-normal map;
+- stable island-ID map;
+- directional slope mask;
+- axis height mask;
+- triplanar texture projection into UV texture space.
+
+The rasterizer is bounded to 4,194,304 output pixels and 32,000,000 triangle-bounding-box pixel tests per invocation.
+
+Triplanar projection samples explicit X/Y/Z images (or one shared image), wraps source texture coordinates, blends them by absolute interpolated world normal, and writes an ordinary precision raster. It is a baked UV-space texture operation, not a runtime shader node or renderer claim.
+
+## Creative Flow proof
+
+The Wave 11 selftest uses the unchanged Creative Flow spine to execute:
+
+`cube -> weld -> UV layout -> sharp seams -> island projection -> pack -> world-normal texture map`
+
+The seven steps cross the existing mesh registry and the new Wave 11 registry without any material-specific planner path.
+
+## Explicit non-claims
+
+Wave 11 does not claim:
+
+- LSCM/ABF/conformal unwrap;
+- optimized UV island packing;
+- angular stretch optimization;
+- high-volume overlap detection beyond the declared bound;
+- automatic seam intelligence or aesthetic seam placement;
+- Substance Painter/Designer smart-material inference;
+- physically simulated dirt/wear;
+- renderer shader graphs;
+- ray-traced baking, curvature, AO or thickness from geometry;
+- live dependency anchors;
+- arbitrary projection-camera/decal perspective solving;
+- texture or material artistic quality judgment.
+
+Those can be added as later executable organs without changing the UV-layout/material-stack contracts established here.
